  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 128 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn upper-level ridge will slowly build across the region through \nFriday, keeping conditions dry through the weekend. High pressure \ncontinues to dominate the region through Saturday, keeping \nsubsidence at the surface. Low level moisture from the continued \nmelting snowpack bring another slight potential for fog \ndevelopment/low stratus tonight, particularly along the river \nvalleys. A dry mid level shortwave passing through today over the \nRocky Mountain Front has brought windy conditions along the \nimmediate eastern foothills this morning. These winds will \ndecrease throughout the afternoon.\n\nThis ridge flattens out heading into Saturday, bringing increased \nwesterly flow aloft. A strong 700mb jet sets up along the Rocky \nMountain Front Saturday afternoon, which coinciding with peak day \ntime mixing, will mix these stronger winds to the surface. The core \nof the jet does not extend much east of the Rocky Mountain Front,\nso the plains will see lesser wind speeds. The increased chinook \nwinds also help push temperatures up back to above normal \nSaturday.\n\nEnsembles hint at a trough coming down from British Columbia during\nthe first half of next week. However, the degree of \ntroughing/timing still varies greatly so details remain \nuncertain. Though, the forecast is pointing towards a cool down \nafter Saturday with some precipitation chances heading into next \nweek.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nModels are still consistently showing the 700mb winds peaking at 60-\n75 kts along the immediate eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain \nFront Saturday afternoon into evening. There remains some \nuncertainty in how far east these core winds extend, with some \nmodels extending it toward the Cut Bank to Sweet Grass Hills area \nwhile some keep the stronger winds to the immediate foothills of \nthe Rocky's. Timing remains uncertain as well. If the peak mid \nlevel jet arrives in the mid afternooon then there will be a \nbetter chance for gusty winds. Some models trend it later in the \nevening, which will lower the chances of these winds mixing to the\nsurface. Ensembles show a 40% chance for 58 mph wind gusts at Cut\nBank and a 30-60% chance for 75 mph wind gusts along the \nimmediate Rocky Mountain Front foothills. -Wilson",
